## Class Structure

### Defining a Class

A class is a blueprint for creating objects. It defines what data the objects hold (instance variables) and what actions they can perform (methods).

```java
public class Student {
    // Instance variables (fields)
    private String name;
    private int grade;
    private double gpa;

    // Constructor
    public Student(String name, int grade, double gpa) {
        this.name = name;
        this.grade = grade;
        this.gpa = gpa;
    }

    // Methods
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public double getGpa() {
        return gpa;
    }
}
```

### Instance Variables

Instance variables (fields) hold the data for each object. They should be declared `private` to enforce encapsulation:

```java
private String name;      // each Student has its own name
private int grade;
private double gpa;
private boolean isActive;
```

### Constructors

A **constructor** initializes a new object. It has the same name as the class and no return type:

```java
public Student(String name, int grade, double gpa) {
    this.name = name;
    this.grade = grade;
    this.gpa = gpa;
}
```

If you do not write any constructor, Java provides a **default constructor** with no parameters that sets fields to default values (0, false, null). But once you write any constructor, the default is no longer provided.

### The `this` Keyword

`this` refers to the current object. It is used to:
1. Distinguish instance variables from parameters with the same name.
2. Call another constructor from a constructor.

```java
public Student(String name, int grade, double gpa) {
    this.name = name;     // this.name = instance variable
    this.grade = grade;   // name = parameter
    this.gpa = gpa;
}

// Constructor chaining
public Student(String name) {
    this(name, 9, 0.0);  // calls the three-parameter constructor
}
```

### Access Modifiers

| Modifier | Accessible From |
|----------|----------------|
| `public` | Anywhere |
| `private` | Only within the same class |
| `protected` | Same class and subclasses |
| (default) | Same package |

**AP CSA rule of thumb:** Instance variables are `private`. Methods are `public` (unless they are helper methods, which are `private`).

### Creating Objects

```java
Student alice = new Student("Alice", 11, 3.85);
Student bob = new Student("Bob", 10, 3.42);

System.out.println(alice.getName());    // "Alice"
System.out.println(bob.getGpa());       // 3.42
```

### Multiple Constructors (Overloading)

```java
public class Rectangle {
    private double width;
    private double height;

    // Two-parameter constructor
    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    // Square constructor
    public Rectangle(double side) {
        this(side, side);     // calls the two-parameter constructor
    }

    // Default constructor
    public Rectangle() {
        this(1, 1);           // 1x1 rectangle by default
    }
}
```

### Common Mistakes

**Mistake 1: Giving the constructor a return type.** Constructors have NO return type (not even void).

**Mistake 2: Forgetting `this.` when parameter names shadow field names.** Without `this.name = name`, you are assigning the parameter to itself.

**Mistake 3: Making instance variables public.** This breaks encapsulation. Always use `private` with getters/setters.

### Practice Exercises

1. Design a `BankAccount` class with fields for owner and balance, a constructor, and getter methods.
2. Write two constructors for a `Circle` class (one with radius, one default with radius 1).
3. Explain what happens if you forget the `this.` prefix in a constructor.
4. Why should instance variables be `private`?


### Deeper Understanding

Object-oriented programming in Java revolves around four pillars: encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction. Encapsulation bundles data and methods together while hiding internal details through access modifiers like private and public. Inheritance creates hierarchical class relationships using the extends keyword. Polymorphism allows treating objects through parent type references while calling overridden methods. Abstraction hides complexity through abstract classes and interfaces.

Arrays are fixed-size zero-indexed collections created with the new keyword. ArrayLists from java.util are dynamic resizable collections that store objects only. Two-dimensional arrays are arrays of arrays traversed with nested for loops in row-major or column-major order.

Sequential search checks each element and has O(n) time complexity. Binary search halves the search space each step with O(log n) time but requires sorted input. Selection sort finds the minimum and swaps it to the front repeatedly with O(n squared) time. Insertion sort shifts elements to insert each value in its correct position with O(n squared) worst case but O(n) best case on nearly sorted data. Merge sort divides the array recursively and merges sorted halves with O(n log n) time in all cases.

Recursive methods must have a base case to stop recursion and a recursive case that calls itself with simpler input. Missing the base case causes StackOverflowError. Trace recursive calls by drawing the call stack. Every recursive solution can be converted to an iterative one and vice versa.

Boolean short-circuit evaluation means the AND operator stops evaluating if the first operand is false and the OR operator stops if the first is true. De Morgans Laws state that NOT of A AND B equals NOT A OR NOT B. String comparison must use the equals method rather than the double-equals operator which only checks reference equality.

