## Traversing Arrays

### For Loop Traversal

The standard way to traverse an array:

```java
int[] scores = {85, 92, 78, 95, 88};

for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
    System.out.println("Score " + i + ": " + scores[i]);
}
```

### Enhanced For Loop (For-Each)

Simpler syntax when you do not need the index:

```java
for (int score : scores) {
    System.out.println(score);
}
```

**Limitations of for-each:**
- Cannot modify array elements (`score = 100` changes the local copy, not the array)
- Cannot access the index
- Cannot traverse backwards
- Cannot skip elements

### When to Use Which

Use **standard for loop** when you need:
- The index
- To modify elements
- To traverse backwards
- To compare adjacent elements

Use **for-each** when you only need:
- To read each element

### Common Traversal Algorithms

**Sum:**
```java
int sum = 0;
for (int val : arr) {
    sum += val;
}
```

**Average:**
```java
double avg = 0;
for (int val : arr) {
    avg += val;
}
avg /= arr.length;
```

**Find maximum:**
```java
int max = arr[0];
for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > max) {
        max = arr[i];
    }
}
```

**Count elements matching a condition:**
```java
int count = 0;
for (int val : arr) {
    if (val > threshold) {
        count++;
    }
}
```

**Check if all elements match:**
```java
boolean allPositive = true;
for (int val : arr) {
    if (val <= 0) {
        allPositive = false;
        break;     // no need to check further
    }
}
```

**Check if any element matches:**
```java
boolean hasNegative = false;
for (int val : arr) {
    if (val < 0) {
        hasNegative = true;
        break;
    }
}
```

### Backwards Traversal

```java
for (int i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}
```

### Partial Traversal

```java
// Print only the first half
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length / 2; i++) {
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}
```

### Comparing Adjacent Elements

```java
// Check if array is sorted
boolean sorted = true;
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
        sorted = false;
        break;
    }
}
```

### Common Mistakes

**Mistake 1: `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.** Using `i <= arr.length` instead of `i < arr.length`.

**Mistake 2: Trying to modify elements with for-each.** `for (int x : arr) { x = 0; }` does NOT change the array.

**Mistake 3: Comparing adjacent elements with `i + 1` without stopping at `length - 1`.** The last element has no next element.

