## Arrays of Objects

### Storing Objects in Arrays

Arrays can hold object references just like they hold primitives:

```java
String[] names = new String[3];
names[0] = "Alice";
names[1] = "Bob";
names[2] = "Carol";

Student[] roster = new Student[5];
roster[0] = new Student("Alice", 11, 3.85);
roster[1] = new Student("Bob", 10, 3.42);
```

### Default Values: null

Uninitialized object array elements are `null`:

```java
Student[] roster = new Student[5];
// roster[0] through roster[4] are all null
System.out.println(roster[0]);        // null
// roster[0].getName();               // NullPointerException!
```

Always check for null before calling methods on object array elements:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < roster.length; i++) {
    if (roster[i] != null) {
        System.out.println(roster[i].getName());
    }
}
```

### Traversing Object Arrays

```java
Student[] roster = {
    new Student("Alice", 11, 3.85),
    new Student("Bob", 10, 3.42),
    new Student("Carol", 12, 3.91)
};

// For-each loop
for (Student s : roster) {
    System.out.println(s.getName() + ": " + s.getGpa());
}

// Standard for loop (when you need the index)
for (int i = 0; i < roster.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(i + ": " + roster[i].getName());
}
```

### Processing Object Arrays

**Find the student with the highest GPA:**
```java
Student best = roster[0];
for (int i = 1; i < roster.length; i++) {
    if (roster[i].getGpa() > best.getGpa()) {
        best = roster[i];
    }
}
System.out.println("Best: " + best.getName());
```

**Count students meeting a condition:**
```java
int honorCount = 0;
for (Student s : roster) {
    if (s.getGpa() >= 3.5) {
        honorCount++;
    }
}
```

### Parallel Arrays (Avoid When Possible)

**Parallel arrays** store related data in separate arrays with matching indices:

```java
// Parallel arrays -- harder to maintain
String[] names = {"Alice", "Bob", "Carol"};
int[] ages = {17, 16, 17};
double[] gpas = {3.85, 3.42, 3.91};
```

This is fragile -- adding/removing elements requires updating all arrays consistently. **Use an array of objects instead:**

```java
// Better: array of objects
Student[] roster = {
    new Student("Alice", 17, 3.85),
    new Student("Bob", 16, 3.42),
    new Student("Carol", 17, 3.91)
};
```

### Comparing Objects in Arrays

When comparing objects, use methods rather than `==`:

```java
// Find a student by name
for (Student s : roster) {
    if (s.getName().equals("Alice")) {
        System.out.println("Found: " + s);
    }
}
```

### Polymorphism with Object Arrays

Arrays can hold subclass objects (covered more in inheritance):

```java
// If Dog and Cat extend Animal:
Animal[] pets = new Animal[3];
pets[0] = new Dog("Rex");
pets[1] = new Cat("Whiskers");
pets[2] = new Dog("Buddy");

for (Animal a : pets) {
    System.out.println(a.speak());  // calls the right version
}
```

### Common Mistakes

**Mistake 1: NullPointerException.** Calling methods on null elements.

**Mistake 2: Comparing objects with `==`.** Use `.equals()` for content comparison.

**Mistake 3: Confusing the array reference with object references.** Each element is a separate reference that can be null independently.

