## ArrayList Traversal

### For Loop Traversal

```java
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<String>();
names.add("Alice"); names.add("Bob"); names.add("Carol");

for (int i = 0; i < names.size(); i++) {
    System.out.println(i + ": " + names.get(i));
}
```

### Enhanced For Loop

```java
for (String name : names) {
    System.out.println(name);
}
```

Same limitations as with arrays: cannot modify the list, cannot access the index.

### ConcurrentModificationException

You **cannot** add or remove elements during a for-each loop:

```java
// CRASHES: ConcurrentModificationException!
for (String name : names) {
    if (name.equals("Bob")) {
        names.remove(name);    // modifying list during for-each!
    }
}
```

### Safe Removal: Backwards Traversal

When removing elements, traverse **backwards** to avoid skipping:

```java
// CORRECT: backwards traversal
for (int i = names.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (names.get(i).equals("Bob")) {
        names.remove(i);
    }
}
```

**Why forward removal fails:**
```java
// BUGGY: forward traversal with removal
// List: ["A", "B", "B", "C"]
for (int i = 0; i < names.size(); i++) {
    if (names.get(i).equals("B")) {
        names.remove(i);
        // After removing index 1: ["A", "B", "C"]
        // i becomes 2, skips the second "B" at index 1!
    }
}
```

**Fix 1:** Traverse backwards (shown above).
**Fix 2:** Decrement `i` after removal: `names.remove(i); i--;`

### Common Traversal Patterns

**Find maximum:**
```java
int max = nums.get(0);
for (int val : nums) {
    if (val > max) max = val;
}
```

**Filter into new list:**
```java
ArrayList<Integer> evens = new ArrayList<Integer>();
for (int val : nums) {
    if (val % 2 == 0) {
        evens.add(val);
    }
}
```

**Transform each element:**
```java
for (int i = 0; i < names.size(); i++) {
    names.set(i, names.get(i).toUpperCase());
}
```

### While Loop Traversal

Useful when size changes during iteration:

```java
int i = 0;
while (i < names.size()) {
    if (names.get(i).length() < 3) {
        names.remove(i);
        // don't increment i -- next element shifted into current position
    } else {
        i++;
    }
}
```

### Common Mistakes

**Mistake 1: ConcurrentModificationException.** Never add/remove during for-each.

**Mistake 2: Skipping elements during forward removal.** Use backwards traversal or adjust the index.

**Mistake 3: Using `==` for object comparison.** Use `.equals()` when comparing ArrayList elements.

### Practice Exercises

1. Remove all even numbers from an ArrayList of Integers.
2. Write a method that returns a new ArrayList with duplicates removed.
3. Demonstrate the skipping bug with forward removal, then fix it.
4. Transform an ArrayList of Strings to title case (first letter uppercase, rest lowercase).


### Deeper Understanding

Object-oriented programming in Java revolves around four pillars: encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction. Encapsulation bundles data and methods together while hiding internal details through access modifiers like private and public. Inheritance creates hierarchical class relationships using the extends keyword. Polymorphism allows treating objects through parent type references while calling overridden methods. Abstraction hides complexity through abstract classes and interfaces.

Arrays are fixed-size zero-indexed collections created with the new keyword. ArrayLists from java.util are dynamic resizable collections that store objects only. Two-dimensional arrays are arrays of arrays traversed with nested for loops in row-major or column-major order.

Sequential search checks each element and has O(n) time complexity. Binary search halves the search space each step with O(log n) time but requires sorted input. Selection sort finds the minimum and swaps it to the front repeatedly with O(n squared) time. Insertion sort shifts elements to insert each value in its correct position with O(n squared) worst case but O(n) best case on nearly sorted data. Merge sort divides the array recursively and merges sorted halves with O(n log n) time in all cases.

Recursive methods must have a base case to stop recursion and a recursive case that calls itself with simpler input. Missing the base case causes StackOverflowError. Trace recursive calls by drawing the call stack. Every recursive solution can be converted to an iterative one and vice versa.

Boolean short-circuit evaluation means the AND operator stops evaluating if the first operand is false and the OR operator stops if the first is true. De Morgans Laws state that NOT of A AND B equals NOT A OR NOT B. String comparison must use the equals method rather than the double-equals operator which only checks reference equality.

