## ArrayList Algorithms

### Searching

**Linear search in an ArrayList:**
```java
public static int search(ArrayList<String> list, String target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
        if (list.get(i).equals(target)) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```

### Inserting in Sorted Order

```java
public static void insertSorted(ArrayList<Integer> sorted, int value) {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < sorted.size() && sorted.get(i) < value) {
        i++;
    }
    sorted.add(i, value);
}
```

### Removing Duplicates

```java
public static ArrayList<String> removeDuplicates(ArrayList<String> list) {
    ArrayList<String> unique = new ArrayList<String>();
    for (String item : list) {
        if (!unique.contains(item)) {
            unique.add(item);
        }
    }
    return unique;
}
```

### Merging Two Sorted Lists

```java
public static ArrayList<Integer> merge(ArrayList<Integer> a, ArrayList<Integer> b) {
    ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<Integer>();
    int i = 0, j = 0;

    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {
        if (a.get(i) <= b.get(j)) {
            result.add(a.get(i++));
        } else {
            result.add(b.get(j++));
        }
    }
    while (i < a.size()) result.add(a.get(i++));
    while (j < b.size()) result.add(b.get(j++));

    return result;
}
```

### Selection Sort on ArrayList

```java
public static void selectionSort(ArrayList<Integer> list) {
    for (int i = 0; i < list.size() - 1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;
        for (int j = i + 1; j < list.size(); j++) {
            if (list.get(j) < list.get(minIndex)) {
                minIndex = j;
            }
        }
        // Swap
        int temp = list.get(i);
        list.set(i, list.get(minIndex));
        list.set(minIndex, temp);
    }
}
```

### Converting Between Arrays and ArrayLists

```java
// Array to ArrayList
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<Integer>();
for (int val : arr) {
    list.add(val);
}

// ArrayList to array
int[] newArr = new int[list.size()];
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    newArr[i] = list.get(i);
}
```

### Practical Example: Word Frequency

```java
public static void wordFrequency(String text) {
    String[] words = text.split(" ");
    ArrayList<String> unique = new ArrayList<String>();
    ArrayList<Integer> counts = new ArrayList<Integer>();

    for (String word : words) {
        int idx = unique.indexOf(word);
        if (idx == -1) {
            unique.add(word);
            counts.add(1);
        } else {
            counts.set(idx, counts.get(idx) + 1);
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < unique.size(); i++) {
        System.out.println(unique.get(i) + ": " + counts.get(i));
    }
}
```

### Common Mistakes

**Mistake 1: Not using `.equals()` for comparison.** `contains()` and `indexOf()` use `.equals()` internally, which is correct. But manual comparisons need `.equals()`.

**Mistake 2: Modifying the list during for-each traversal.** Use index-based loops or backwards traversal.

**Mistake 3: Off-by-one in insertion.** `list.add(i, value)` inserts BEFORE index i.

