## Subclass & Superclass

### What Is Inheritance?

**Inheritance** is an object-oriented mechanism where a new class (subclass/child) acquires the attributes and methods of an existing class (superclass/parent). It represents an **is-a** relationship.

```java
// Superclass
public class Animal {
    private String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }

    public String speak() {
        return name + " makes a sound";
    }
}

// Subclass
public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);          // call superclass constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }

    public String getBreed() { return breed; }
}
```

### The `extends` Keyword

`public class Dog extends Animal` means Dog inherits from Animal. Dog gets ALL public (and protected) methods and fields from Animal.

### Constructor Chaining with `super()`

A subclass constructor MUST call the superclass constructor:

```java
public Dog(String name, String breed) {
    super(name);     // MUST be the first statement
    this.breed = breed;
}
```

If you do not call `super()` explicitly, Java automatically inserts `super()` (no-argument version). If the superclass has no no-argument constructor, the compiler gives an error.

### Accessing Inherited Members

```java
Dog rex = new Dog("Rex", "Labrador");

// Inherited from Animal:
System.out.println(rex.getName());    // "Rex"
System.out.println(rex.speak());      // "Rex makes a sound"

// Dog-specific:
System.out.println(rex.getBreed());   // "Labrador"
```

### Private Members Are Not Directly Accessible

A subclass inherits private fields but cannot access them directly -- it must use the public getters/setters:

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {
    public void printInfo() {
        // System.out.println(name);    // ERROR: name is private in Animal
        System.out.println(getName());  // OK: using the public getter
    }
}
```

### The `is-a` Relationship

A Dog **is an** Animal. A subclass can be used anywhere the superclass is expected:

```java
Animal a = new Dog("Rex", "Lab");   // Dog IS an Animal
a.getName();      // works (inherited)
// a.getBreed();  // ERROR: Animal doesn't know about getBreed()
```

### Inheritance Hierarchy

```java
class Vehicle { ... }
class Car extends Vehicle { ... }
class ElectricCar extends Car { ... }
```

ElectricCar inherits from Car, which inherits from Vehicle. ElectricCar has all members from both Car and Vehicle.

### Common Mistakes

**Mistake 1: Forgetting `super()`.** If the superclass has no no-arg constructor and you do not call `super(args)`, it is a compile error.

**Mistake 2: `super()` must be the first line.** You cannot do anything before calling `super()` in a constructor.

**Mistake 3: Accessing private superclass fields directly.** Use getters instead.

